Ahiru, the owner's file manager on Zenix (roadmap block 3: "the owner's Ahiru file manager ... ported"; DESIGN D27
section 9; docs/d27-c-library.md, "Ahiru on Zenix"; the user's page is docs/man/ahiru.txt)

What it is: a file manager in the way of ranger and yazi that draws in the terminal: three columns (the parent, the
folder, a preview of what the cursor is on), vi keys with prefix keys and a panel of what can follow, marks, yank and
paste, tabs, find, archives and disk images opened as folders, written for AmigaOS over the owner's CFile core (core/:
the formats; fs/: folders, archives, copy, move, delete, rename with the safety rules) and tui.library. On Zenix it
is /bin/ahiru, a program with shared code (ZEX2: a second Ahiru costs only its data), linked with the text-UI
library's pure build (build/libtui-pure.a, ports/tui) and the C library's (build/libc-pure.a).

upstream/  the owner's source, one source for AmigaOS and Zenix (D27 9, rule 1; owner 2026-10-28), pinned here: the
           owner's CFile repository (private), commit 78e80fd4559c0bfd427783eba407648930029c81 (2026-10-17), Ahiru
           0.3b6 and the CFile core it builds: ahiru/src/ (Ahiru's screen, keys, previews, operations), core/ and fs/
           (the core's files Ahiru links, and the headers of the parts Zenix replaces), src/paula.h, ahiru/README.md as
           ahiru/README (the user's documentation, which docs/man/ahiru.txt follows). The owner approved
           (2026-10-28) publishing Ahiru and the core in Zenix's tree under MIT; the CFile repository's files carry no
           licence notice, so tools/ahiru-vendor.sh puts a two-line MIT notice naming him at the top of each copied
           source file (Zenix's copy only, otherwise unchanged) and LICENSE is his MIT licence (the wording of his
           other MIT projects): the notice that travels with the program (tools/mkroot.sh and the package ahiru put
           it in /usr/share/licenses/ahiru, with the text-UI library's as ahiru-tui).
zenix/     Zenix's own files (GPL-2.0-only):
           os.c       the core's OS layer (fs/os.h) on Zenix, instead of upstream's fs/os.c (AmigaOS's ExAll,
                      assigns, the DosList, 1.3's packets): a folder read with readdir and lstat (a soft link is
                      ST_SOFTLINK: never walked by a copy or a delete, the core's rule; a hard link one more name of
                      a file), the volume list the mount table (SYS_mountinfo: "/" and every mount), free bytes
                      from the mount's filesystem, Unix wildcards (fnmatch; "#?" read as "*"), dates set with
                      SetFileDate, os_launch through the shell in the background
           none.c     the parts with no place on Zenix's text console yet, each answering as upstream does when its
                      library or device is missing: sound (src/paula.c and the players: no program touches the
                      chips; the sound server is block 6), picture previews (thumb.c: the console has no pixels of
                      its own), ARexx (rexx.c), the commodity and Workbench (fs/cx.c), ADF images in trackfile.device
                      drives (fs/mount.c: an ADF still opens as a folder), loading the archive code when used
                      (fs/modload.c: Ahiru is one ZEX2 here, the archive code always in), the background paste
                      (jobs.c: no threads, no fork; D27 9 rule 3: a paste runs in the front with its bar and Esc)
           term.c     upstream's ahiru/src/term.c, compiled unchanged with one answer of Zenix's: every terminal is
                      taken for CTerm's (the text-UI library's CTerm 1: the ANSI colours as they are), so MODE auto
                      opens inside it; Zenix's console has no screens or windows (ports/common/gui.c)
           list.c     upstream's ahiru/src/list.c, compiled unchanged with the parent of a Unix path: "/tmp" goes up
                      to "/", "/" to the volume list (upstream's AmigaDOS shapes would take "/tmp" straight there)
           ops.c      upstream's fs/ops.c, compiled unchanged with one check of Zenix's before a paste: a picked
                      archive member or disk image folder named "..", "." or "" refuses the paste with a message
                      (on AmigaDOS ".." is an ordinary name; on Zenix "TARGET/.." is the target's parent: the full
                      audit FA-11, B3-6). Inside a pasted folder upstream's safename leaves such names out already
           (term.c, list.c and ops.c rename upstream's function by a define and declare it static first, so it is
           compiled and only the Zenix wrapper is called, as ports/tui/zenix/core.c does)

The AmigaOS layer is ports/common (shared with Sumi, Maki and Markdown, no copy): dos.c compiled with the names Ahiru
maps ("Ahiru": PROGDIR:Ahiru.keys, .theme, .config, .places, .marks as $HOME/.config/Ahiru/Ahiru.*, the folder made
mode 700; "FileColours": ENVARC:FileColours as $HOME/.config/FileColours, Maki's), and gui.c, the screen calls,
which never open. What each call does is in ports/common/README; the ones Ahiru is the first to need: more places
(SYS: as /, T: and RAM: as /tmp/T-UID, NIL: as /dev/null), locks with absolute paths and NameFromLock resolving soft
links, ParentDir, CurrentDir, Info, DateStamp from 1978 (AmigaOS's, which the core counts in) and SetFileDate,
IoErr's AmigaOS numbers, AvailMem, Wait on the terminal (TuiSignal's bit, ports/tui) and the reply ports, Execute
and SystemTags in the background, FPuts, AllocMem.

Ahiru's own (for the main session to confirm with the layer's choices):
- Inside the terminal only (CLAUDE.md: no desktop features in the text console): MODE screen or window, or SCREEN or
  WINDOW on the command line, end with "cannot open a screen or window"; so does a start without a terminal.
- Unix modes and owners: the core's protection is the Unix mode (bits 8-19 and bit 31, as the layer's Examine gives
  it) with AmigaOS's r w e bits set when this user may not (a write-protected target is refused as upstream refuses
  one) and h for a dot file (Ahiru's hidden colour); a copy keeps the mode and the date (SetProtection, SetFileDate),
  root's saves keep the owner (the layer's SetProtection), a copy by root is root's (as cp without -p). The status
  line and line mode show AmigaOS's letters hsparwed from those bits (upstream's protstr): what this user may do,
  not the mode itself.
- Links: a soft link is listed as a link (its colour), shown as what it points to, left out of a copied folder
  (upstream's rule, a count in the summary), deleted as a link, never walked by a delete.
- Mounts: the volume list (h from "/", gv) is the mount table; = d (an ADF into a drive) says there are no drives for
  disk images on Zenix; Enter on an ADF, ISO, lha, lzx or zip opens it as a folder (the core's arcfs). Changing an lha,
  lzx or zip archive needs C:lha, C:lzx or C:zip, which Zenix does not have: those archives are read only (unpack
  with x, copy out with yank and paste); ADF images are written by the core itself.
- E: the editor is $EDITOR, else Ahiru.config's EDITOR, else upstream's C:Ed, which the layer runs as /bin/sumi (the
  owner's editor): in the terminal, Ahiru waiting, then drawn again. ! and S (a command, a shell) run in the
  terminal; S runs $SHELL, else /bin/sh ("EndShell goes back" is upstream's word: exit).
- Patterns (s find, f filter, cr rename by pattern): Unix wildcards (* ? [...]); "#?" still means *. Names match by
  case for patterns; upstream's own name comparisons (the cursor's name, a target already there) ignore case, as
  AmigaOS does (below).
- The stack: STACK_DEFAULT (16 KB). -fstack-usage: the largest frames 2.5 KB (pack), 2.1 KB (op_openfile), under
  1.7 KB the rest; the recursive walks (deltree 336 bytes, copytree 176, treestat 164, find's walk 212) stop at the
  core's MAXDEPTH 20, and a Zenix path has at most 255 bytes: the deepest chain (smain, run, fs_delete, 20 deltrees)
  is about 12 KB. Upstream swaps to a 64 KB stack of its own on AmigaOS; FindTask answers a task that needs none.

Sizes (FS-UAE, the stock A1200, 2026-10-30): /bin/ahiru 242,876 bytes: 228,632 of code shared by every Ahiru (the
CFile core's half of it: core/ about 58 KB, fs/ about 72 KB before linking, the archive and disk-image code
included), 8,026 of data and 41,518 of BSS per process. Memory with /tmp/a open on 80 x 32 (ps): Ahiru owns 178 KB
and shares 225 KB of code (chip RAM free 1,331 KB before, 826 KB with Ahiru, the test's shell and ps); 392 KB before
the allocator of 2026-10-30 (docs/d27-c-library.md "The allocator").

Found in upstream (for the owner's repository, not changed here):
- Memory: 178 KB of data for one Ahiru; besides the code: its BSS (41 KB: the preview card 7.5 KB, the key bindings
  3 KB, ...), the 16 KB preview buffer (PBUFSZ + 16 bytes), two 64 KB buffers while it copies, the text-UI library's
  grids (31 KB). (It was 392 KB with the power-of-two allocator: measured block by block since, docs/d27-c-library.md
  "The allocator".)
- Paths are AmigaDOS shapes in the core and Ahiru: "g/" (this volume's root) finds no ':' and stays; "dt" (the
  trashcan, <volume>:Trashcan) finds no ':' and does nothing, without a word; the title row puts a '/' between the
  folder and the name unless the folder ends in ':', so at the root it reads "//bin"; the default keys go to SYS:
  C: LIBS: DEVS: ENV: T: RAM: (on Zenix SYS:, T: and RAM: are mapped; C:, LIBS:, DEVS: and ENV: say they cannot go
  there). A path layer in the core (the volume's root, joining, the parent: AmigaOS's or Unix's) would make them
  right on both.
- Names compare without case (nocase: dl_find, place_find, the collision check before a paste): on a filesystem whose
  names keep their case, pasting README beside readme asks to replace a file that is not there (and then writes a
  file of its own: nothing is lost).
- A picture inside an archive is unpacked to T: for its preview before thumb_make is asked (preview.c): where no
  picture can be shown (a plain console, Zenix's) the unpacking is work for nothing.
- "O" (open with) and launch keys start the program in the background with no terminal (os_launch: NIL: on
  AmigaOS, /dev/null here): right for a program with a window of its own, not for one that lives in the terminal.
- The background paste needs a second process sharing the core's memory (CreateNewProc); a system without threads
  needs a paste done in slices from the main loop for it.
- Messages and help keep AmigaOS words ("EndShell goes back", "MultiView", the keys file's ENVARC: and PROGDIR:
  names), as Sumi's do.
- Replaced files: core/lha.c, core/lzx.c and their headers are not upstream's: they were derived from LHa for UNIX and unlzx, and
  never come here (the owner's CFile repository still has them). Zenix's clean readers (user/arcread.h, user/arclha.c,
  user/arclzx.c; DESIGN D33) serve the same calls, GPL-2.0-only; core/lha.h and core/lzx.h are two lines leading to them. A member
  whose name may not be written makes the listing fail: the archive opens as damaged with what came before it.
- Licences, for the owner to confirm (the notice added here names him for every file, as approved): core/dms.c is
  "a port of xDMS 1.3 (public domain)". core/ptplay.c (a port of 8bitbubsy's PT2.3F replayer) is not copied (no sound here).

Tests: tools/ahiru-test.sh (FS-UAE, the stock A1200; in make check): ahiru opens /tmp/a, the grid read back with
termtest screen (CON_READ) at each step, the keys typed with CON_INPUT: a copy into a folder, a move (cut and paste),
a delete at its question, E opening Sumi on a file and Ahiru drawn again after it, q; the files checked on the disk
(the copy byte for byte with its mode, the move, the delete, the link left alone), the settings folder and
FileColours; the memory printed.

To update: tools/ahiru-vendor.sh /path/to/CFile [COMMIT] (the files above from that commit, the notice added), put
the commit here, build, run tools/ahiru-test.sh, and bring docs/man/ahiru.txt in step with the README's changes; a
file upstream adds to Ahiru's build goes into the lists in tools/ahiru-vendor.sh and the Makefile (AHIRU_*); if
upstream starts to use an AmigaOS call amigados.h does not have, the build says so (an undeclared function):
ports/common gets it.
